{"data":{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.12483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.12483","prefix":"10.48550","suffix":"arxiv.2605.12483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2605.12483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"alternateIdentifiers":[{"alternateIdentifierType":"arXiv","alternateIdentifier":"2605.12483"}],"creators":[{"name":"Xu, Yuanda","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanda","familyName":"Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Sang, Hejian","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hejian","familyName":"Sang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Zhou, Zhengze","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengze","familyName":"Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"He, Ran","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Wang, Zhipeng","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Geramifard, Alborz","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alborz","familyName":"Geramifard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond GRPO and On-Policy Distillation: An Empirical Sparse-to-Dense Reward Principle for Language-Model Post-Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"lang":"en","subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"lang":"en","subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-12T17:57:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:27:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-14T15:02:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T01:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T15:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:58:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-20T14:15:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-12T03:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:53:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"ris":"GEN","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"article","schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceType":"Article","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0","rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"In settings where labeled verifiable training data is the binding constraint, each checked example should be allocated to the model and reward density where it is most informative. We identify a reward-density principle: sparse sequence-level reward is most useful on models that can explore and discover better behavior, while dense token-level teacher supervision is better suited for compressing that behavior into a smaller deployment model; accordingly, scarce labeled data should be used upstream on the strongest available teacher, then transferred downstream as dense supervision. We evaluate this rule through a four-stage workflow:teacher RL, forward-KL warmup, on-policy distillation, optional post-bridge student RL on verifiable math and code generation with Qwen3, Llama, and Qwen2.5 models. At fixed Qwen3-1.7B deployment-student size, an RL-improved 8B teacher distilled through the dense bridge outperforms direct GRPO on the same student ($79.3\\%$ vs.\\ $75.9\\%$ on MATH; $25.2\\%$ vs.\\ $19.8\\%$ on AIME~2024), while transfer from the same teacher before RL underperforms. A component ablation confirms that each stage is load-bearing: the RL-improved teacher adds $7.8$ absolute points on MATH over a raw teacher of the same size, the forward-KL warmup adds $1.7$, and on-policy distillation adds $3.3$. The teacher-quality ordering: raw-teacher transfer $\u0026lt;$ direct GRPO $\u0026lt;$ RL-teacher transfer, replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct with a Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct teacher and on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct for code generation with a Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct teacher. The operational lesson is to avoid spending scarce labeled data on the least prepared policy: use sparse reward for teacher-side discovery, dense transfer for student compression, and student-side sparse reward only after the bridge. The OPD implementation used in our experiments is available at https://github.com/HJSang/OPSD_OnPolicyDistillation.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"viewsOverTime":[],"downloadCount":0,"downloadsOverTime":[],"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"citationsOverTime":[],"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-05-13T02:50:35.000Z","registered":"2026-05-13T02:50:36.000Z","published":"2026","updated":"2026-10-14T08:33:31.000Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}},"provider":{"data":{"id":"arxiv","type":"providers"}},"media":{"data":{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2605.12483","type":"media"}},"references":{"data":[]},"citations":{"data":[]},"parts":{"data":[]},"partOf":{"data":[]},"versions":{"data":[]},"versionOf":{"data":[]}}}}